      use: 'Definite articles point to a specific thing, like “the” in English. Le before a masculine singular noun (le livre), la before a feminine singular noun (la table), les before every plural of either gender (les garçons, les filles). Before a noun that begins with a vowel sound — a, e, i, o, u, y — or a silent h, le and la both shrink to l’: l’ami, l’ordinateur, l’homme, l’heure, l’enfant, l’arbre, l’oiseau.',
      avoid: 'Never write “le ami” or “la école” — the elision to l’ is compulsory in the singular. L’ hides the gender, so memorise it separately: l’orange is feminine, l’ananas is masculine. In the plural nothing shrinks: les amis, les écoles. And les is not optional — English drops the article in “I like apples”, French cannot: j’aime les pommes.',
