      use: 'A possessive agrees with the thing owned, not with the owner. Masculine singular takes mon, ton, son; feminine singular takes ma, ta, sa; every plural takes mes, tes, ses. Notre, votre and leur serve both genders in the singular and become nos, vos, leurs in the plural.',
      avoid: 'Son and sa do not tell you the owner’s sex — sa voiture is his car or her car, depending on context. Before a vowel or silent h the feminine switches to the masculine form for sound: mon amie, mon école, ton histoire — never “ma amie”. And leur before a noun never takes an s in the singular: leur maison, but leurs amis.',
